{
  "concept": "A polar rectangle is the region bounded by two constant-r circular arcs and two constant-θ rays — the polar analogue of a Cartesian rectangle, whose 'sides' are curved because holding r constant traces an arc, not a line.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Origin (pole) at lower-left with a horizontal black ray drawn along the polar axis (θ = 0)",
    "Inner circular arc r = 1.5, solid blue (thick), spanning beyond the two bounding rays at both ends",
    "Outer circular arc r = 2, solid blue (thick), spanning beyond the two bounding rays at both ends",
    "Lower bounding ray θ = π/6, dashed red, emanating from origin and extending past the outer arc",
    "Upper bounding ray θ = π/4, dashed red, emanating from origin and extending past the outer arc",
    "Shaded curved quadrilateral region R (light blue fill) between the two arcs and two rays",
    "Italic label 'R' centered inside the shaded region",
    "Labels 'r = 1.5' and 'r = 2' with short leader placement at the upper-left ends of the two blue arcs",
    "Labels 'θ = π/6' and 'θ = π/4' placed near the two dashed red rays, closer to the origin",
    "Optional overlay: straight-sided Cartesian-style rectangle of dimensions Δr × (r·Δθ) anchored at the inner-lower corner of R, drawn as a dashed gray outline",
    "Optional overlay annotations: 'Δr' bracket along a radial edge and 'r Δθ' bracket along the inner arc",
    "Readout box showing current r1, r2, θ1, θ2 and the exact area ½(r2² − r1²)(θ2 − θ1)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "innerRadius",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Inner radius r₁",
      "teaches": "The inner boundary of R is a circular arc of constant radius; moving it changes the radial thickness Δr while the angular wedge stays fixed.",
      "range": [0.25, 2.75, 0.05],
      "default": 1.5
    },
    {
      "id": "outerRadius",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Outer radius r₂",
      "teaches": "The outer boundary is a second constant-r arc; the region always lies between two concentric arcs, and its area grows faster as r₂ increases because arc length scales with r.",
      "range": [0.5, 3.0, 0.05],
      "default": 2.0
    },
    {
      "id": "startAngle",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Start angle θ₁ (× π)",
      "teaches": "The straight sides of R are rays of constant θ; rotating this ray sweeps R around the pole without changing its radial bounds.",
      "range": [0, 0.45, 0.01],
      "default": 0.1667
    },
    {
      "id": "endAngle",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "End angle θ₂ (× π)",
      "teaches": "Widening Δθ stretches the arcs but leaves the radial edges straight and the same length — Δr and Δθ are independent dimensions of the polar rectangle.",
      "range": [0.05, 0.5, 0.01],
      "default": 0.25
    },
    {
      "id": "cartesianOverlay",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Overlay straight-sided rectangle (Δr × r Δθ)",
      "teaches": "Why R is only an analogue of a rectangle: a true rectangle of the same Δr and r Δθ matches R at the inner corner but pulls away from the outer arc, because the arc length r Δθ grows with r.",
      "default": false
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The polar rectangle R",
      "narration": "R is the set of points whose radius lies between 1.5 and 2 and whose angle lies between π/6 and π/4. Two solid blue arcs and two dashed red rays bound it.",
      "state": { "innerRadius": 1.5, "outerRadius": 2.0, "startAngle": 0.1667, "endAngle": 0.25, "cartesianOverlay": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Change the radial bounds",
      "narration": "Push the outer arc outward: R thickens radially while the two rays — and therefore the angular extent — stay exactly where they were.",
      "state": { "innerRadius": 1.5, "outerRadius": 2.8, "startAngle": 0.1667, "endAngle": 0.25, "cartesianOverlay": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Shrink Δr toward zero",
      "narration": "Bringing the arcs together turns R into a thin curved strip. In the limit this is the dr in the double integral — the radial slice of the region.",
      "state": { "innerRadius": 1.85, "outerRadius": 2.0, "startAngle": 0.1667, "endAngle": 0.25, "cartesianOverlay": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Change the angular bounds",
      "narration": "Now hold the radii fixed and swing the rays apart. R sweeps through a wider wedge; its straight radial edges keep the same length, but the arcs get longer.",
      "state": { "innerRadius": 1.5, "outerRadius": 2.0, "startAngle": 0.05, "endAngle": 0.42, "cartesianOverlay": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare with a true rectangle",
      "narration": "Overlay a straight-sided rectangle Δr tall and r₁Δθ wide at the inner corner. It hugs R near the inner arc but falls short of the outer arc — the outer side is longer, r₂Δθ.",
      "state": { "innerRadius": 1.5, "outerRadius": 2.0, "startAngle": 0.1667, "endAngle": 0.25, "cartesianOverlay": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why dA = r dr dθ",
      "narration": "Shrink Δr and Δθ and the mismatch vanishes: R looks like a rectangle of sides Δr and r Δθ, so its area approaches r Δr Δθ — the polar area element.",
      "state": { "innerRadius": 1.85, "outerRadius": 2.0, "startAngle": 0.20, "endAngle": 0.25, "cartesianOverlay": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A polar rectangle is 'rectangular' only in the (r, θ) plane — in the xy-plane its two constant-r sides bow into arcs whose lengths r₁Δθ and r₂Δθ differ, and that stretching factor r is exactly why dA = r dr dθ instead of dr dθ.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Pole at the lower-left of the canvas with a solid black horizontal polar axis running right from it. Two dashed red rays rise from the pole at θ₁ ≈ 30° and θ₂ ≈ 45°, extending well past the outer arc. Two thick solid blue circular arcs centered at the pole at r = 1.5 and r = 2 cross both rays and overshoot them at each end, so the four boundary curves visibly extend beyond the region. The enclosed curved quadrilateral is filled pale blue with a thin blue edge and labeled with an italic serif 'R' near its center. Radius labels 'r = 1.5' and 'r = 2' sit up and to the left near the top ends of the arcs; angle labels 'θ = π/4' and 'θ = π/6' sit just left of / below the corresponding dashed rays, nearer the pole. Scale the drawing so r = 3 still fits on canvas. The optional overlay is a dashed gray straight-sided rectangle anchored at the inner-lower corner of R, aligned with the radial direction, with height Δr and width r₁Δθ, plus small bracket annotations 'Δr' and 'r Δθ'. A small readout in a corner displays current r₁, r₂, θ₁, θ₂ (as multiples of π) and the exact area ½(r₂² − r₁²)(θ₂ − θ₁)."
}

